 \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"3f3021bc3f62035004ee04d1f64745704205af4e485d209bb2cf6fc3a0a9a840","event_uuid":"14018ec2-148c-428b-9329-4fad297b087d","position":{"commit_sha":"d2c15204c89502a6ac6f18bb1cff14e47954dc06","position":"1787174716333926001-14018ec2-148c-428b-9329-4fad297b087d"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"1f341386c5a3a1672b883bcb13c59c552327a62551c562a07adc98d6cd4560e6","event_uuid":"ca9112df-80b7-4f57-96e7-7e92114672a0","position":{"commit_sha":"ea650f7e99a19328e4f9e5a6ee09901f4582a6df","position":"1787174910950059858-ca9112df-80b7-4f57-96e7-7e92114672a0"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"7bf4872a30375819d79362dc3d5f81f3a0dcd091b5eaeea43d1836bdf05de08b","event_uuid":"d8a5e7ce-f31d-495d-85ac-042bc29074d2","position":{"commit_sha":"5e370a28756bdf9c009b233fc6fccd6f9f7b3922","position":"1787176615974287001-d8a5e7ce-f31d-495d-85ac-042bc29074d2"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":"claude-code_2-1-235_agent","claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":"eff19c3d-071a-47b5-bae6-8b48a3343a8c","comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Archaeology (why the existing gate missed these): scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py (policy d37e, commit 6739ea54fa) passes CLEAN on the current tree. Its policy sanctions git_adapter.py/_ref_lock.py at MODULE granularity, so callers composing the exported primitives (git_adapter.add at reconcile_helpers.py:226 + git_adapter.commit at :246 — no lock, no atomic commit+push) are approved call-by-call. Gate enforces the mechanism rule (no raw subprocess git) but not the protocol rule (mutations via the atomic transaction). Fix = R3 composition rule in the EXISTING checker; description rewritten accordingly.","timestamp":1786808315626180001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Sequencing (operator, 2026-08-15): land the R3 gate AFTER the parallel session's remediations land — cubiform-wooden-fruitbat (bridge-state writes) and gnatty-comparable-ynambu (binding-store snapshot + alert_store) — recorded as blocked_by links. Rationale: landing R3 first would require reasoned markers on exactly the sites those tickets are deleting (merge-conflict churn in reconcile_helpers.py and friends); landing after means R3's first run should enumerate ZERO out-of-transaction sites (or only ones with a durable reason), making the gate land clean with no debt baseline. Do not claim until both blockers close.","timestamp":1786808435547542001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Sequencing (operator, 2026-08-15): land the R3 gate AFTER the parallel session's remediations land — cubiform-wooden-fruitbat (bridge-state writes) and gnatty-comparable-ynambu (binding-store snapshot + alert_store) — recorded as depends_on links (blocking). Rationale: landing R3 first would require reasoned markers on exactly the sites those tickets are deleting (merge-conflict churn in reconcile_helpers.py and friends); landing after means R3's first run should enumerate ZERO out-of-transaction sites (or only ones with a durable reason), so the gate lands clean with no debt baseline. Do not claim until both blockers close.","timestamp":1786808464910924001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"R3 dry-run enumeration (2026-08-15, grep sweep of all sanctioned-primitive callers): (1) reconcile_helpers.py:226/246 git_adapter.add+commit — covered by blocker gnatty-comparable-ynambu. (2) bridge-state projects_store + alert_store._atomic_write — covered by blocker cubiform-wooden-fruitbat. (3) ONE additional site NOT covered by either blocker: rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:315-355 legacy .reconciler-* lock-file purge — composes read_tree/rm_cached/write_tree/commit_tree/update_ref in a DETACHED temp index with a CAS ref-advance. Concurrency-safe by construction (never touches worktree/index; CAS makes concurrent writers safe), so it needs NO pre-gate remediation: disposition at implementation time is either a durable reasoned marker (detached-index CAS migration) or promotion into git_adapter as a named transaction helper — or outright deletion if the legacy-lock migration is complete by then. (4) _commands/{txn,compact_txn,compact_recovery,delete,init}.py run_git_write callers are store-seam transaction internals per policy d37e — they join R3's sanctioned entry set, not remediation. (5) Workflows clean: only 'git worktree add' setup lines; zero raw-git-ok markers in .github/workflows or scripts/*.sh.","timestamp":1786808765735495001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Session wind-down state (2026-08-15 13:55 PT): still parked on ONE dependency — gnatty-comparable-ynambu (6454-d06e, in_progress): its remediation change Gerrit 1821 ('reconciler: route writes through store seams') is NEW with Verified -1 on ps1, being fixed by the owning session. cubiform-wooden-fruitbat closed (change 1813 merged). Sequencing rationale re-confirmed mechanically: R3 would flag reconcile_helpers.py's composed add/commit sites in its own CI run if landed before 1821 merges. Next session: when ynambu closes, run review-plan -> claim -> implement R3 per the description (dry-run enumeration + site dispositions already recorded in earlier comments).","timestamp":1786827825416737001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"R3 dry-run enumeration RE-RUN on origin/main @ 0b851c8103 (2026-08-19), both blockers now CLOSED (fea4-f98c bridge-state writes; 6454-d06e binding-store/alert_store parity). Result: TWO out-of-transaction composition sites remain, BOTH with a durable reason — not remediation debt.\n\nSITE 1 — src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile_helpers.py:226/246 (_commit_binding_store_snapshot: git_adapter.add + git_adapter.commit). Blocker 6454-d06e was originally scoped to convert this and DID NOT: its closed description carries a 'Scope note — binding-snapshot conversion was ATTEMPTED and REVERTED (behavior-preserving boundary)'. Evidence recorded there: routing it through push.commit_and_push_tickets_branch (the only offered commit seam, which stages with 'git add -A') sweeps in .bridge_state/last-pass.json, which the deliberate selective 5-file staging excludes, so the tickets HEAD advances on every idempotent pass and tests/integration/rebar_reconciler/test_reconcile_idempotency.py::test_unchanged_second_pass_preserves_binding_store_bytes_and_head goes RED (reproduced in CI on change 1821 ps1 and locally). Preserving the exact committed set through the seam needs EITHER a seam pathspec parameter OR a write_lock-wrapped selective-commit helper — both descoped. DISPOSITION: durable reasoned inline marker citing that boundary, plus a discovered_from follow-up ticket owning the seam-pathspec gap so the debt is visible in the tracker, not only in-tree.\n\nSITE 2 — src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:338-351 (_purge_committed_reconciler_locks: read_tree/rm_cached/write_tree/commit_tree/update_ref). Confirmed on the current tree exactly as the earlier dry-run described: a DETACHED temp index (GIT_INDEX_FILE=<repo>/.git/reconciler-purge-index) plus a CAS ref-advance (update_ref with old_value). It never touches the worktree or the main index, and the CAS is the concurrency control. DISPOSITION: durable reasoned inline marker. Rejected the alternative 'exempt detached-index operations as a rule' — GIT_INDEX_FILE arrives as an env dict built at the call site, so detachedness is not statically decidable from the callable alone, and a blanket rule would be trivially forgeable by any future caller passing env=; a per-site reasoned marker keeps the sanction explicit and auditable. Rejected 'promote into git_adapter as a named transaction helper' — one caller, legacy migration slated for deletion; a new seam export would outlive its only use.\n\nALSO CORRECTED from the plan text: commit_email is NOT a mutation primitive. git_adapter.commit_email is 'git log -1 --format=%ae' — a READ despite the name. Including it in R3's primitive set would be a false-positive generator. R3's mutation set is add, commit, commit_tree, read_tree, rm_cached, update_ref, write_tree.","timestamp":1787173328143723001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Implementation pushed for review: Gerrit change 1916 (commit 7ad468f867, Change-Id Iffdc5d03b43bc68e0e42fec2e820aa3a1d0ea72d), branch r3-composition-gate off origin/main 0b851c8103.\n\nR3's FIRST RUN on the real tree enumerated exactly 7 hits across 2 functions, with ZERO false positives anywhere else in src/ + scripts/ (the riskiest-assumption check from the plan): __main__.py:338-351 read_tree/rm_cached/write_tree/commit_tree/update_ref, and reconcile_helpers.py:226/246 add/commit. Both took a durable reasoned function-level marker as planned; no debt baseline, no allowlist file.\n\nLocal gates: make lint exit 0 (complexity ratchet active=49 new=0 increased=0); make typecheck exit 0 over 568 files; tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py 49 passed (up from 42 — 7 new R3 tests plus the parametrized seam-file case and the repo-tree regression); tests/unit/rebar_reconciler 2945 passed; scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py exits 0 on the tree and --report lists all 7 seam-composition hits with their reasons.\n\nOne plan detail corrected during implementation: an empty binding set is NOT sufficient to disable R3 inside the seam files, because the module NAME alone binds a receiver. _SeamBindings therefore carries an explicit 'enabled' flag that the seam-internal files switch off. Caught by the parametrized sanctioned-entry test, which failed on the first implementation pass.","timestamp":1787174610611195001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Correction to the plan's Context section, recorded after the plan-review E4 advisory (which was right) and fixed in the commit message on patchset 2 of Gerrit 1916.\n\nThe plan said 'every mutation primitive in git_adapter.py carries a function-level # raw-git-ok: marker'. Only FOUR do: rm_cached (git_adapter.py:184), update_ref (:230), add (:246), commit (:252). read_tree, write_tree and commit_tree carry none — and the reason is itself evidence for R3: R1/R2 only fire on verbs in MUTATION_VERBS ({add, commit, rm, mv, reset, update-ref, checkout, clean}), and 'read-tree'/'write-tree'/'commit-tree' are NOT in that set. Those three primitives were therefore invisible to the pre-R3 gate entirely, not merely sanctioned by it. That widens the original archaeology rather than narrowing it: __main__.py's detached-index purge composed five primitives, THREE of which no rule could ever have seen.\n\nThe description text is left as-is deliberately — editing it now would invalidate the signed plan-review attestation for a wording fix; the accurate statement lives here and in the landed commit message.","timestamp":1787174716333926001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Patchset 2 took Verified -1: the module-size gate. My reasoned marker on _purge_committed_reconciler_locks pushed rebar_reconciler/__main__.py from 798 to 804 LOC, over the locked 800 cap. Not environmental, so no recheck. Operator authorized splitting the file (verified: not in any open ticket's file_impact, not covered by an RP epic).\n\nSEAM CHOSEN — the pass-lock lifecycle, extracted whole to src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_pass_lock_lifecycle.py: _lock_steal_enabled (steal kill-switch), _resolve_held_lock (steal / re-acquire resolution), _purge_committed_reconciler_locks + _LEGACY_LOCK_FILES (legacy lock-file migration), _acquire_or_adopt_pass_lock. One existing call-graph cluster, not a mechanical slice — _post_pause_preflight consults _lock_steal_enabled, whose answer decides whether _resolve_held_lock runs, whose returned OID _acquire_or_adopt_pass_lock adopts. It also matches how the package is already laid out (_advisory_lock.py = ref backend, _ref_lock.py = CAS/lease primitives, _heartbeat.py = lease renewal, _preflight.py = visibility guard); the orchestrator-side lock sequencing was the piece still inlined in __main__.\n\nSIZES: __main__.py 804 -> 704 (below the 720-LOC band the gate itself warns at, so real headroom, not another 798); _pass_lock_lifecycle.py 133. Under the 200-500 target on purpose: that IS the cluster, and padding it with unrelated functions to reach 200 would be the mechanical carving the policy forbids — the binding constraint is the 100-LOC floor. .github/module-size-limit.txt untouched.\n\n_post_pause_preflight deliberately stayed in __main__: it returns a _Disposition, and _Disposition / PassResult / _finish_disposition are the module's shared result vocabulary (used by the pass-execution path too, and by _bridge_runner.py:152 + tests/scripts/test_run_reconcile_bridge_heldout.py:201), so moving them into the lock module would invert the dependency.\n\nPATCH TARGETS PRESERVED: __main__ imports the four moved callables into its own namespace, so main() still reads them as module globals. Verified via grep-as-string, not just Serena — the suite patches them by name: patch.object(main_mod, '_purge_committed_reconciler_locks') in test_bridge_request_heldout.py:40 and test_bridge_selection_heldout.py:66, monkeypatch.setattr in test_reconciler_last_pass_heldout.py:101 and test_bridge_status.py:165, main_mod._lock_steal_enabled / _resolve_held_lock in test_lock_steal.py, main_mod._purge_committed_reconciler_locks in test_advisory_lock_ref_backend.py. _LEGACY_LOCK_FILES is NOT re-exported (nothing outside the moved code referenced it).\n\ndocs/env-vars.md regenerated: gen_env_registry.py records the file a var is read from, and REBAR_RECONCILER_LOCK_STEAL now lives in _pass_lock_lifecycle.py. That generator-staleness test was the only other local failure the move caused.\n\nNice side effect: the split is an end-to-end check of R3 itself. The purge function's 5 seam-composition hits followed it into the new module, still sanctioned by the marker that travelled with the function, and _pass_lock_lifecycle is NOT in R3's sanctioned entry set — so the rule still applies there rather than being silently switched off by the move.\n\nGates after the split: make lint exit 0 (complexity ratchet active=49 new=0 increased=0), make typecheck exit 0 over 569 files, check_raw_git_writes.py exit 0, 3018 tests passed across tests/unit/rebar_reconciler + the lint tests + the bridge heldout script tests. Pushed as patchset 3 of Gerrit 1916 (commit 26b000d3c7); the push outdated the prior LLM-Review +1, so both votes are re-running.","timestamp":1787176615974287001}],"created_at":1786808157806881001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"b8f7bc95-a70e-46c4-96d1-4245e7c80794","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"6ccc-0577-198c-44fa"},{"link_uuid":"8b9564a4-af02-432f-90b7-5a85f3b591a0","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e72e-259d-5ee7-4e73"},{"link_uuid":"77eb020d-085d-4beb-aa20-a38b2f49075b","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"fea4-f98c-f1a2-4168"},{"link_uuid":"c0e14e4d-e489-4d85-8642-acf05dcb4a98","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"6454-d06e-7361-4e3d"},{"link_uuid":"ca9112df-80b7-4f57-96e7-7e92114672a0","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"7c8b-75d6-7489-4a61"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\n`scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` (policy `d37e-4f64-3265-4f30`, wired at `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:172`) passes CLEAN on the current tree, yet the reconciler still mutates the tickets tracker outside the atomic locked transaction. It misses those writes by construction: rules R1 (raw `subprocess` git) and R2 (the wrapper names `run_git`/`run_git_write`/`_run_git`/`_git`) enforce the MECHANISM rule \"no raw subprocess git\", while the reconciler write seam is sanctioned at MODULE granularity — every mutation primitive in `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/git_adapter.py` carries a function-level `# raw-git-ok: reconciler write seam (git_adapter/_ref_lock)` marker (`git_adapter.py:184`, `:230`, `:246`, `:252`). Because those primitives are sanctioned exports, ANY caller may compose them into a hand-rolled non-atomic sequence and the checker approves each call individually. The PROTOCOL rule — \"tracker mutations go through the atomic transaction\" — is unenforced. Proven live instance: `reconcile_helpers.py:226`/`:246` (`git_adapter.add` + `git_adapter.commit`) stage-and-commit against the tickets tracker with no lock and no atomic commit+push.\n\n## Approach\nAdd rule **R3 (seam composition)** to the EXISTING checker rather than a new script, so one gate, one marker vocabulary and one CI wiring keep covering raw git writes. R3 re-scopes the sanction from \"every export of the wrapper module\" to \"the transaction seams themselves\".\n\nR3 fires on a call to a git-adapter **mutation primitive** made from outside the reconciler write-seam modules. The primitive set is `add`, `commit`, `commit_tree`, `read_tree`, `rm_cached`, `update_ref`, `write_tree`. Read/query exports are excluded, and so is `commit_email`: despite its name it is `git log -1 --format=%ae` (`git_adapter.py:293`), a read.\n\nBecause `add`/`commit` are ubiquitous method names, R3 binds on the RECEIVER, not the bare attribute: a call fires only when the receiver is a resolved git-adapter module reference. Two binding forms, both already used in-tree, are resolved per file — an import binding, `from rebar_reconciler import git_adapter` (`__main__.py:321`, `apply_outbound.py:67`, `_concurrency.py:55`), including `as`-aliases; and a dynamic-loader binding, `git_adapter = _load(\"rebar_reconciler.git_adapter\", \"git_adapter.py\")` (`reconcile_helpers.py:203`), recognised by the module-path string argument. A bare-name call also fires when the name was `from`-imported out of a git-adapter module. Anything else is not a git-adapter call and does not fire, so `set.add(...)` and `session.commit(...)` stay silent.\n\nSanctioned entry set (R3 never fires inside these): the reconciler write-seam files themselves — `git_adapter.py`, `_ref_lock.py`, `_ref_lock_push.py`. Those ARE the atomic transaction; the primitives are defined and composed there under the ref lock.\n\nSanction elsewhere reuses the existing inline `# raw-git-ok: <reason>` marker unchanged — line-level or enclosing-function, reason mandatory, no external allowlist file. Reusing it rather than minting a second marker keeps one sanction vocabulary, and the existing `marker-without-reason` violation then covers R3 for free.\n\n**Riskiest assumption, checked first:** that R3's receiver binding yields no false positives across `src/` and `scripts/` — the rule is unusable if ordinary `.add(`/`.commit(` calls fire. The cheapest falsifying check is `--report` over the tree, confirming every new hit is a real git-adapter call; it runs before any marker is written.\n\n## Scope\n- `scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` — add R3: the primitive set, the per-file git-adapter binding resolver, the seam-internal file set, the emission, and the docstring policy. R1/R2, marker semantics and the fail-closed classes stay unchanged.\n- `tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py` — R3 unit tests against synthetic trees.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile_helpers.py` — durable reasoned marker on `_commit_binding_store_snapshot`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py` — durable reasoned marker on `_purge_committed_reconciler_locks`.\n\n## Site dispositions (from the rule's first run; full evidence in a ticket comment)\nBoth remaining out-of-transaction sites take a **durable reasoned marker**, not a \"drains later\" debt baseline.\n\n1. `reconcile_helpers.py:226/246` — `_commit_binding_store_snapshot`. Ticket `6454-d06e-7361-4e3d` was scoped to convert this and deliberately did not: routing through `rebar._store.push.commit_and_push_tickets_branch` (which stages `git add -A`) sweeps in `.bridge_state/last-pass.json`, which the selective 5-file staging excludes, advancing the tickets HEAD every idempotent pass and turning `test_reconcile_idempotency.py::test_unchanged_second_pass_preserves_binding_store_bytes_and_head` RED. The marker cites that boundary; follow-up `11a9-b11b-e93d-4832` owns giving the seam a pathspec so the marker can be deleted.\n2. `__main__.py:338-351` — `_purge_committed_reconciler_locks`. Composes the primitives in a DETACHED temp index (`GIT_INDEX_FILE` set at `__main__.py:337`) and CAS-advances the ref (`update_ref(..., new_commit, old)`), so it never touches the worktree or main index and a concurrent writer is safe. Per-site marker rather than a blanket \"detached-index is exempt\" rule: detachedness comes from an `env` dict built at the call site, so it is not statically decidable from the callable, and a blanket rule would be forgeable by any future caller passing `env=`.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tests in `tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py`, driven against synthetic source trees as the existing tests are:\n- **Fires:** a module doing `from pkg import git_adapter` then `git_adapter.add(d, \"f\")` and `git_adapter.commit(d, \"m\")` yields two `seam-composition` violations naming those primitives.\n- **Sanctioned entry:** the same calls inside `git_adapter.py` / `_ref_lock.py` / `_ref_lock_push.py` yield zero R3 violations.\n- **Marker semantics:** those calls with `# raw-git-ok: reason` on the line, and with it on the enclosing function, both pass; a bare `# raw-git-ok:` still fails as `marker-without-reason`.\n- **False-positive floor:** `s = set(); s.add(x)` and `session.commit()` in a module with no git-adapter binding yield zero violations, as do `git_adapter.rev_parse(...)` and `git_adapter.commit_email(...)`.\n- **Dynamic-loader binding:** `git_adapter = _load(\"rebar_reconciler.git_adapter\", \"git_adapter.py\")` then `git_adapter.commit(...)` fires.\n- **Whole-tree regression:** `check(REPO_ROOT)` returns no violations with the markers in place.\n\n## Non-goals\nRemediating the two sanctioned sites, changing `git_adapter`'s API, and runtime interception.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] R3 fires on a git-adapter mutation-primitive call outside the seam; verified by the new unit tests passing (`pytest tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py`).\n- [ ] R3 does not fire inside `git_adapter.py`, `_ref_lock.py` or `_ref_lock_push.py`; verified by the sanctioned-entry unit test.\n- [ ] R3 does not fire on non-git-adapter `.add(`/`.commit(` calls or on read-only git-adapter exports; verified by the false-positive-floor unit test.\n- [ ] A reasoned `# raw-git-ok:` (line or enclosing function) sanctions an R3 hit and a reasonless marker still fails; verified by the marker unit tests.\n- [ ] Both enumerated sites carry a durable reasoned inline marker stating a concrete justification, not a placeholder; verified by reading the two diffs and by `python scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` exiting 0.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py --report` lists every R3 hit with its reason and `check` reports zero unsanctioned violations.\n- [ ] `make lint` (incl. the complexity ratchet) and `make typecheck` exit 0.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] The change lands via Gerrit at `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`; the operator records the change number and both vote outcomes as a ticket comment.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py","reason":"add composition rule R3"},{"path":"tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py","reason":"R3 tests"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile_helpers.py","reason":"inline reasoned markers on existing composition sites"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["depends_on","6454-d06e-7361-4e3d"],["depends_on","fea4-f98c-f1a2-4168"],["relates_to","7c8b-75d6-7489-4a61"],["relates_to","e72e-259d-5ee7-4e73"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"5ba84ad7-ad33-49be-b072-433147fab401","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"in_progress","tags":[],"ticket_id":"638a-3746-e58a-4929","ticket_type":"task","title":"Gate: shrink-only ratchet forbidding tracker git mutations outside the _store seam","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["326649b0-e173-4eb1-8eee-e2ba1a3b1c07","209e4628-e085-4b64-a340-6c747a47cd7e","c7d5d8a9-5476-4b78-915f-c96db3e7bad2","6ee0df9d-f85f-4613-bff0-ced70df09acc","959014d6-e577-41c6-a3cd-6d6794efd473","ad564ada-4269-4917-a312-c96e86bc234a","d80e15f2-1255-4195-854d-afaedaa83c2c","c1b60cfa-1a26-4b9c-a928-e33ec8146351","ea837037-e1d3-45eb-8c81-80ed843171f0","ad8d14af-a117-4bdc-8cb3-3eb6231e0971","5ba84ad7-ad33-49be-b072-433147fab401","665e7222-0916-45e7-b688-833b598971a9","93727812-e7df-4af1-aa8e-402e4fe75782","14018ec2-148c-428b-9329-4fad297b087d","ca9112df-80b7-4f57-96e7-7e92114672a0","d8a5e7ce-f31d-495d-85ac-042bc29074d2"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787176615974287002,"uuid":"f87e92db-ab25-423e-ae1a-491066129755"}